The clause is about a total difference compared with 50.00 EUR: at or under it, skip the extra review; above it, ask for one review. The only candidate that matches this is the one about an invoice whose total difference is above 50.00 EUR. The clause does not say "invoice", but the candidate names the total difference and the same threshold, so I count it as a match.

The single-line difference candidate is a different condition, since it concerns one line rather than the total. Every other candidate concerns shipments, sessions, helper agents, approvals, suppliers or carriers, none of which the clause mentions.

{"answers":[false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,true,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false]}

The clause's only trigger is that the total difference on an invoice exceeds 50 euros. Only that trigger gets "true"; the other candidates describe different situations.

Question 11 asks about a total difference above 50.00 EUR, which is the same condition, so it is true. Question 13 concerns a single line difference, which is not the total, so it is false.

{"answers":[false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,true,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false]}

Only two of the candidate situations match the clause's trigger, which is an invoice file about to be edited: "An invoice file is about to be edited?" (index 10) is a direct match, and "just before editing an invoice file" is the same situation. "An invoice is about to be deleted?" is a different action, so it does not count.

Every other candidate is false.

